 "transcript": {
  "answer": "**Short answer:** Credit card rates sit about 17 percentage points above the cash rate. Personal loan rates can't be compared with current data because the RBA's series ends in February 2020.\n\n**Credit cards (standard, advertised)**\n- September 2026: **21.24%** p.a., against a cash rate of **4.36%**. The gap is about **16.9 points**.\n- January 2022: 19.94% against a cash rate of 0.10%, a gap of about 19.8 points.\n- Since January 2022 the cash rate has risen 4.26 points, but card rates have risen only 1.30 points.\n- The card rate barely responds to cash rate moves. It stayed at 20.99% through the 2025 cuts (cash rate 4.35% to 3.60%), and it rose only 0.25 points in September 2026, when the cash rate was already back at 4.36%.\n\n**Personal loans (unsecured, fixed)**\n- The RBA's F5 table has the fixed personal loan series only up to **February 2020**, at **12.46%**, when the cash rate was much lower. That series shows no data after that date, so I can't give a current personal loan rate or a reliable current spread from these sources.\n- The variable personal loan series also ends in February 2020, at 14.41%.\n\n**Bottom line:** Credit cards are priced far above the cash rate and move only slightly with it. The most recent personal loan figures I can get are from before the 2022–23 tightening cycle, so I can't say how they compare today.",
